Filtering Blue Cars from a Car Array
Frequent mistakes
1.	Using == instead of .equals()
if (car.getColor() == "blue")
Problem:
•	Compares references, not content
•	Very common beginner error
Correct:
car.getColor().equalsIgnoreCase("blue")
________________________________________
2.	Not handling case sensitivity
equals("blue")
Problem:
•	Fails for "Blue" or "BLUE"
________________________________________
3.	Why getBlueCars(Car[] cars) can not be part of the class implementation? why this methods must be in the main? Is there any alternative solution to implement a method inside the class and call it from main class with a parameter of Car color?
1️.	 Short Answer
getBlueCars(Car[] cars) does NOT belong inside the Car class because a Car object represents one car, not a collection of cars.
Putting that method in Main was convenient, not mandatory.
________________________________________
2️.	Why It Does NOT Belong in Car
Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)
Car → represents ONE car
Filtering → requires MULTIPLE cars
A Car object:
•	Knows its own color
•	Does not manage other cars
So this would be conceptually wrong:
public class Car {
    public static Car[] getBlueCars(Car[] cars) { ... } // ❌ bad design
}
Why?
•	A single Car should not filter other Car objects
•	Violates encapsulation and responsibility
________________________________________
3️.	Why It Was Put in Main
Because:
•	Main is a driver / procedural controller
•	Acceptable place for logic in beginner labs
•	Easy to understand and grade
But it is NOT the only correct design.
________________________________________
4️.	BEST Object-Oriented Alternative (Recommended)
Create a Manager / Utility Class
This is the correct OO solution.
CarUtils.java
Main.java
Car[] blueCars = CarUtils.getCarsByColor(cars, "blue");
✔ Clean
✔ Reusable
✔ Follows OOP
